            <ArticleTitle>Multiple coronary aneurysms, ectasia, and myocardial infarction evaluated by multidetector row CT in a young adult.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e16-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijcard.2009.01.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although coronary aneurysms and ectasia are well-recognized, they are uncommon sequelae and may evolve into myocardial infarction. When such a case is suspected, conventional coronary angiography and echocardiography are the most commonly used imaging modalities. Here we report a case of non-specific vasculitis in a 31-year-old woman presenting with multiple coronary aneurysms, ectasia, and myocardial infarction identified by multidetector row computed tomography.</AbstractText>
